Docker is a platform that allows you to build, package, and run applications inside containers.
A container is a lightweight, isolated environment that contains everything your app needs to run—without affecting or depending on the host system. 

Container → the box that holds everything [ code , libraries etc ] your app needs to run. It is the running instance of docker image. 

1. A Dockerfile is a text file containing instructions that Docker uses to build a Docker image.
2. Docker Images:
IT acts as template from which containers are created. It consists of application code, libraries , dependencies , config files etc 
Built from a Dockerfile, which contains instructions like:
	• Base image
	• App code
	• Dependencies
	• Startup command
3. Docker Containers:
 It is the running instance of the Docker Images 
Containers are:
	• Lightweight
	• Isolated from each other
	• Faster than virtual machines

A container is a lightweight, standalone package that includes:
	• Your application code
	• The runtime (like Python, Node.js, Java, etc.)
	• System tools and libraries it needs
4. Docker Registries:
Storage locations for Docker images, such as Docker Hub.
5. Docker Engine:
The core component that powers Docker. Docker Engine contains - Docker CLI / docker client , Rest API &  Docker Daemon
Docker Engine Components
	• Docker CLI: Acts as the dashboard, letting you issue commands.
	• REST API: Translates CLI commands (e.g., docker run) into HTTP requests for the Docker Daemon. This enables automation and integration with external tools.
	• Docker Daemon: The “engine” that handles pulling images, building containers, and other core functions.


• The Docker Daemon is the core of Docker.
