✅  How resources allocation works for containers?

1. CPU Allocation
docker run --cpu-shares=512 myapp
docker run --cpus="2" myapp   # Max

2. Memory Allocation
docker run --memory="512m"

3. Isolation & Enforcement
	• Namespaces: Isolate processes, network, mounts, etc.

You can set resource limits in docker-compose.yml:

version: '3.8'
services:
  app:
    image: myapp
    deploy:
      resources:
        limits:
          cpus: '1.5'
          memory: 512M
        reservations:
          cpus: '0.5'
          memory: 256M
``


✅ Key Benefits
	• Prevents one container from hogging resources.
	• Enables predictable performance.
	• Helps in multi-tenant environments.


✅ Docker Bloating? 
Docker bloating is when containers carry unnecessary baggage, making them heavier, slower, and more expensive than they need to be.

Docker bloating means your Docker images or containers become much bigger than they need to be, which wastes disk space, slows builds, and makes deployments heavier.



Common causes
	• Using large base images (e.g., ubuntu instead of alpine)
	• Installing build tools (compilers, package managers) that aren’t removed afterward
	• Copying unnecessary files (logs, docs, .git folders)
	• Layer buildup from poorly ordered Dockerfile commands
	• Not cleaning package caches after installs
